you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  the met office issues its first ever  extreme heat warning  as parts of the uk bake in record  temperatures  as european leaders say more action is  needed to tackle global warming  following devastating floods we look at  what could be done  to adapt to our changing climate  and will tokyo 2020 be it the most  sustainable olympics yet  i'll be speaking to athlete and  environmental campaigner paula ratcliffe  hello and welcome to the uk's only  daily climate news show where we track  the changes happening to our world right  now  and we meet those coming up with the  solutions  now the met office will issue its first  ever extreme  heat warning today in the uk as  temperatures in parts of the country  continue to soar extreme heat episodes  in the uk are becoming more frequent and  prolonged  and can lead to increased energy use  wildfires  and problems as well for the health  service well our correspondent melania  veselinovich  joins me now melania what's going on  well the met office has introduced this  warning  together with public health england for  the first time this year in order to  increase preparedness and raise  awareness of the dangers that extreme  heat  can bring to health to infrastructure  some of those things as you mentioned  are increased  traffic to coastal areas people trying  to cool off by the sea perhaps  increased energy consumption using air  conditioning units perhaps which is  something that we hadn't really used in  this country before there's risk of  wildfires there's risk of people  presenting with health  problems related to heat to hospitals  which could put a strain on them and  potentially lead  to premature death so this is all very  concerning because the met  office says that these episodes are  becoming hotter they're becoming  more frequent and more prolonged  we do know that this warning has been  issued for  south wales west midlands south west  england and further east  into hampshire as far as the isle of  wight it is valid from  now until the end of thursday when the  heat is expected to start breaking down  a bit  all right so be careful melania thank  you  now germany's chancellor angela merkel  says that her country must do more to  tackle climate change  that's after record floods caused the  country's worst natural disaster  in nearly six decades as the waters  recede and clean up operations begin  the focus now turns to how to ensure  disasters like this  can't happen again as jonathan samuels  reports  in germany's our valley wine region they  live and work  close to nature they're also at her  mercy  vineyard owner frank josten believes  he's a victim of our reluctance to adapt  to climate change his home his  award-winning winery  his wife's business all but destroyed in  minutes  a community shattered we know a lot of  people  in that house over there and that those  over there  and the in the down level so they died  the people  all the people died chancellor angela  merkel warns  we must adapt to a world where natural  disasters happen with greater frequency  and intensity we have to hurry  we have to get faster in our fight  against climate change  and i believe that the european union is  working on just that  it's a message heard clearly here we are  not safe nature is stronger  than we are yeah and that's that's the  main message and that we see that we can  work with that yeah we have to we have  to find our  our agreements with the nature  how we manage our rivers build our towns  use our land  and conserve water are more crucial than  ever  being proactive rather than reactive  will save money  and lives despite all these regulations  they aren't always followed people still  build in flood plains  is it the same complacency is it the  same lack of awareness which you know  leads us to make the same mistake over  and over again  cutting co2 emissions is key to tackling  climate change  but with earth's temperature already now  above one degree celsius  we have to modify how we live our lives  to avoid pain  like the austin family is going through  jonathan samuels sky news availa  in germany now with the impacts of  climate change  already being felt around the world it's  not enough to just focus  on reducing emissions and limiting  global warming  adaptation and mitigation are important  to allow people to live with our  changing climate  instead of being forced from their homes  by it  for example in the united states miami  beach has been called the ground zero  of sea rise but the rising above program  aims  to mitigate against the worst effects it  includes elevating roads to fit new  drainage systems underneath  building sea walls and using data on  ocean levels to decide the height  of new buildings and intense heat waves  are becoming  more common due to climate change in  paris  a city-wide network of cooling islands  is being built including spaces such as  parks and pools  linked by cool walkways and the alentejo  region  in portugal is experiencing less rain  more frequent droughts  and substantially hotter temperatures  the city of evora  is using underground water sources for  irrigation  while nature-based solutions such as  more trees help provide  shade and improve flood resilience  and two-thirds of bangladesh is less  than  five meters above sea level so it's  vital that people there  find ways to adapt in the village of  kalibogi homes have been lifted onto  bamboo stilts  to help keep them dry and in the uk  a 20-year project on the norfolk broads  is stopping homes and farmland from  flooding  more than 240 kilometers of flood banks  are being upgraded using reused and  reclaimed materials  while a 10 kilometer stretch is being  replaced with  reed banks and lagoons and of course  technology is going to play an  increasingly  key role in fighting climate change and  adapting  to its impacts and today a new climate  action group  called subac has been launched to help  fund high-tech solutions to the climate  crisis  baroness bryony worthington is one of  the founders as well as one of the lead  authors of the 2008 climate change act  and she joins me now baroness  worthington really good  to talk to you i was mentioning the  launch today of  subac but you already have technology  within it don't you that's helping to  combat  climate change yes that's right thank  you  um we've set up an accelerator to help  not-for-profit enterprises who want to  make a difference on climate change to  collaborate  and to gain access to professional  skills so they can become more impactful  and they're calling it the climate subac  it's based on an  old idea of how you need to collaborate  to enable everyone to thrive  and we're really excited we've got five  really exciting organizations that are  kicking us off  as the founding members  when you see the scenes of devastation  across europe with the flooding  at the moment are there technological  solutions that you  envisage being able to help prevent that  kind of thing happening  so we are now into the age of  consequences um scientists have been  warning us  of this for decades and we've been  incredibly slow to act  and now really we've got to double down  on  both predicting how bad things are going  to get how quickly  preventing the worst from happening and  then surviving  through what's inevitable so that kind  of predict prevent and survive motto has  to be ingrained in everything that we do  and we are still you know in the  foothills really of understanding this  problem  it's really complicated the scale of  change that we need to adapt to is going  to be extraordinary and it's all  happening  in a time scale i think that even the  scientists are quite surprised by the  speed so  it's it's quite a serious effort and the  fact that it's taken  severe events in northern hemisphere  countries like canada like germany  to sort of put this on their front  headlines it is quite  astounding because these sorts of  impacts have been coming for many years  but they've perhaps not been taken as  seriously as they should have done so  if this does act as a wake-up call  that's great but we should have really  heeded these warnings a long time ago  okay baroness bryony worthington really  good to talk to you thank you  thank you very much well in today's  other climate news  thousands of hectares of land are  engulfed in smoke in siberia  as authorities tackle 187 forest fires  there  the kremlin has blamed the unprecedented  wildfires on unusually high temperatures  and climate change  the blazes mean that permafrost beneath  the ground is at risk of starting to  thaw  which could release huge amounts of  carbon into the atmosphere  the u.s special presidential envoy for  climate will visit the uk tomorrow  john kerry will make a speech at the  royal botanic gardens q  about the urgency of global climate  action  before traveling on to rome and naples  to attend the g20  ministerial meeting on environment  climate change and energy  he'll then return to london for  discussions about the cop 26 climate  conference  which is being held in glasgow in  november  and formula one driver sebastian vettel  has been photographed  litter-picking after the british grand  prix the four-time  world champion swapped his helmet for  bin bags to help cleaners with the  mammoth task  after 140 000 fans watched yesterday's  race  earlier this month vettel created a bee  hotel and  over the course of the race weekend  talked about his commitment to finding  long-lasting solutions for  sustainability  now the tokyo olympics start this week  and organizers have said that they hope  it will be the most sustainable games  yet  well they're using carbon credits to  offset emissions  claiming that this could mean the first  ever carbon  negative olympics the medals that will  be awarded to  athletes will use precious metals  extracted from used electronics  and competitors will even be sleeping on  cardboard beds  however a study published in the journal  nature found that from 1992 onwards  olympic games have been getting steadily  worse for the environment  as events become bigger and more  ambitious  well olympian and environmentalist paula  radcliffe is hoping that the games will  inspire us not only to get active but  also to go green paula radcliffe joins  me now  hello good to talk to you paula i know  you've been working with  nationwide trying to encourage people to  make their own homes  greener what have you made of the tokyo  games green credentials  well i think it's it's all a step in the  right direction i think  that the awareness amongst the whole  population  but certainly amongst athletes is is  really moving  much more to to appreciate the  environment if anything i think the  pandemic has taught us  to to see how important it is to do what  we can  now to preserve the environment it's for  not for our futures it's for the future  of our grandchildren and our great  grandchildren  and that's why it's a huge honor for me  to be working with nationwide on this  program because  the research that they have done shows  that 76  of us are already not using single-use  plastics anymore  they already know how to change on that  but people just aren't as aware  and i do think it's more of a  misunderstanding of what needs to be  done  in our own homes and maybe an  overestimation of the cost  as well now the tokyo games has a global  stage doesn't it's a great opportunity  to get some green  messaging out there what are some of the  challenges with an event that  size of keeping it green well i think as  you talked about the beginning the  challenge as the olympics have grown is  that people still need to they still  need to congregate in one place  people want to go and watch it um and  then that has to be then  beamed and sent all over the world and  all of that  costs a lot uh in terms of energy  um and so i think this year is certainly  helped if you like by the fact that it  is not a normal olympics there aren't  the numbers traveling out there that  there would normally be  um and so i think that will help to  contribute to making it more sustainable  and just even the fact that we're  thinking about it that making those  small steps  um for me that's always preferable than  doing nothing even if we look at it i  think  it's a huge task to get it anywhere  close to net zero  for for the olympics so let's not even  try i'd rather try and try and do small  amounts and i'm sure  if everybody feels like that then all of  our small amounts together that's why  we're stressing  what we can do yeah it's a marathon not  a sprint as they say paula radcliffe  good to talk to you definitely  thank you well that is everything from  us for today  coming up on the daily climate show  tomorrow how the drinks industry is  going green you can find out more here  tomorrow on  sky news thanks for watching  [Music]  you

